WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.665
The use-case inventory is the only
government-wide list of federal AI,

2
00:00:04.665 --> 00:00:07.337
so the program reads it as identity data.

3
00:00:07.337 --> 00:00:09.425
A few fields are solid anchors.

4
00:00:09.425 --> 00:00:13.900
The use-case ID is stable and never
reused, so it can key a record.

5
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.563
The bureau gives a placement.

6
00:00:15.563 --> 00:00:18.424
And a stage of Retired is a leaver event.

7
00:00:18.424 --> 00:00:23.763
Other fields are only leads; the contact,
for example, may be a public inbox.

8
00:00:23.763 --> 00:00:28.555
But the facts that decide access aren't
there at all: what the system signs in as,

9
00:00:28.555 --> 00:00:32.299
its credential, its permissions,
and the person accountable.

10
00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:36.479
The high-impact fields report status,
not evidence, and in the published

11
00:00:36.479 --> 00:00:40.800
file a large share of deployed high-impact
use cases answered none of them.

12
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.761
And a use case isn't an identity,

13
00:00:42.761 --> 00:00:46.212
so the program records the
link on the directory side.

14
00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:49.990
Next, how to tell what kind
of identity an AI system is.
